					<description><![CDATA[<figure><img width="1024" height="683" src="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?fit=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1" class="attachment-rss-image-size size-rss-image-size wp-post-image" alt="" decoding="async" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?w=1800&amp;ssl=1 1800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=600%2C400&amp;ssl=1 600w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1 1024w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=768%2C512&amp;ssl=1 768w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=1536%2C1024&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=1200%2C800&amp;ssl=1 1200w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=550%2C367&amp;ssl=1 550w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=800%2C533&amp;ssl=1 800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1 780w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=400%2C267&amp;ssl=1 400w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?fit=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1&amp;w=370 370w" sizes="(max-width: 34.9rem) calc(100vw - 2rem), (max-width: 53rem) calc(8 * (100vw / 12)), (min-width: 53rem) calc(6 * (100vw / 12)), 100vw" data-attachment-id="466850" data-permalink="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-beyond-september-events-roundup/attachment/sept1_boots/" data-orig-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?fit=1800%2C1200&amp;ssl=1" data-orig-size="1800,1200" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="Sept1_Boots" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;The giant cowboy boots in San Antonio have become one of the national monuments of Texas along with Big Tex at the State Fair and the San Jacinto Monument in LaPorte.&lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?fit=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1" /></figure>
<p>Famous footwear. Texas claims several entries in the Guinness Book of World Records, but one of the most famous is “The Giant Justins,” otherwise known as the World’s Largest Cowboy Boots. Before the 40-foot-tall and 30-foot-long faux-ostrich boots landed along Loop 410 in San Antonio they were part of Washington Project for the Arts funded [&#8230;]</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-beyond-september-events-roundup/">Day Trips &amp; Beyond: September Events Roundup</a> appeared first on <a href="https://www.austinchronicle.com">The Austin Chronicle</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<figure><img width="1024" height="683" src="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?fit=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1" class="attachment-rss-image-size size-rss-image-size wp-post-image" alt="" decoding="async" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?w=1800&amp;ssl=1 1800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=600%2C400&amp;ssl=1 600w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1 1024w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=768%2C512&amp;ssl=1 768w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=1536%2C1024&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=1200%2C800&amp;ssl=1 1200w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=550%2C367&amp;ssl=1 550w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=800%2C533&amp;ssl=1 800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1 780w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?resize=400%2C267&amp;ssl=1 400w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?fit=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1&amp;w=370 370w" sizes="(max-width: 34.9rem) calc(100vw - 2rem), (max-width: 53rem) calc(8 * (100vw / 12)), (min-width: 53rem) calc(6 * (100vw / 12)), 100vw" data-attachment-id="466850" data-permalink="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-beyond-september-events-roundup/attachment/sept1_boots/" data-orig-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?fit=1800%2C1200&amp;ssl=1" data-orig-size="1800,1200" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="Sept1_Boots" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;The giant cowboy boots in San Antonio have become one of the national monuments of Texas along with Big Tex at the State Fair and the San Jacinto Monument in LaPorte.&lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept1_Boots.jpg?fit=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1" /></figure>
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Famous footwear.</strong> Texas claims several entries in the <strong>Guinness Book of World Records</strong>, but one of the most famous is <strong>“The Giant Justins,”</strong> otherwise known as the <strong>World’s Largest Cowboy Boots</strong>. Before the 40-foot-tall and 30-foot-long faux-ostrich boots landed along Loop 410 in San Antonio they were part of <strong>Washington Project for the Arts</strong> funded by the National Endowment for the Arts. In 1979, Austin native <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bob_Wade_(artist)"><strong>Bob “Daddy-O” Wade</strong></a> made the boots, which turned out to be one of many whimsical art projects he placed <a href="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-bob-daddy-o-wade-11776512/"><strong>around the state</strong></a> before he died in 2019. In 1980, three 18-wheeler trucks carted the steel, wood, urethane foam, and fiber-crete boots to the north entrance of North Star Mall. The sculpture is illuminated every night, but during the Christmas season they are especially colorful.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>World’s Largest Cowboy Boots, 7400 San Pedro Ave., San Antonio, <a href="https://www.northstarmall.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">www.northstarmall.com</a></em></p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" width="780" height="520" data-attachment-id="466851" data-permalink="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-beyond-september-events-roundup/attachment/sept2_spam/" data-orig-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?fit=1800%2C1200&amp;ssl=1" data-orig-size="1800,1200" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="Sept2_Spam" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;Visitors of all ages enjoy the SPAM® Museum in Austin, MN. There are lots of fun facts about the canned lunch meat as well as photo ops and interactive displays.&lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?fit=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1" src="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?resize=780%2C520&#038;ssl=1" alt="" class="wp-image-466851" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?w=1800&amp;ssl=1 1800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?resize=600%2C400&amp;ssl=1 600w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?resize=768%2C512&amp;ssl=1 768w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?resize=1536%2C1024&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?resize=550%2C367&amp;ssl=1 550w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?resize=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1 780w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?resize=800%2C533&amp;ssl=1 800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?resize=400%2C267&amp;ssl=1 400w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept2_Spam.jpg?w=370&amp;ssl=1 370w" sizes="(max-width: 780px) 100vw, 780px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Visitors of all ages enjoy the SPAM® Museum in Austin, MN. There are lots of fun facts about the canned lunch meat as well as photo ops and interactive displays. <span class="image-credit"><span class="credit-label-wrapper">Credit:</span> Gerald E. McLeod</span></figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mystery meat.</strong> It takes a lot of self-confidence for a town to call itself <strong>“SPAMTOWN USA.”</strong> <strong>Austin, Minnesota</strong>, wears the moniker with pride as the hometown of the canned lunch meat. The northern Austin is a handsome little burg of 26,000 with a downtown area that is part antique and part modern (they have a historic <strong>Paramount Theatre</strong>, too). It’s a company town dominated by its largest employer: <a href="https://www.hormelfoods.com/"><strong>Hormel Foods</strong></a>. The centerpiece of the business district is the <a href="https://www.spam.com/museum"><strong>SPAM® Museum</strong></a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The museum is surprisingly small but is a colorful vault of information and hands-on exhibits. The company is a shining example of American ingenuity and innovation with an impressive number of firsts to its credit. Founded in 1891 by <strong>George A. Hormel</strong> as a pork-packing company working directly with local farmers, it was the first to bring canned hams to the market. <strong>Chili con carne</strong> may have been invented by the <strong>Chili Queens</strong> in the street markets of <strong>San Antonio</strong>, but Hormel canned it and spread chili around the world in 1935. The company’s <strong>Dinty Moore</strong> brand of canned ready-to-eat meals introduced in 1935 still feeds millions of college students. <strong>SPAM</strong>, the name supposedly a combination of “spice” and “ham,” was introduced in 1937. Made with only six ingredients, the main parts being pork shoulder and ham, the slurry of blended parts is cooked in the 12-ounce cans. My mother stretched her food budget by serving her six kids fried SPAM with eggs, but more inventive recipes followed the canned lunch meat around the world. The Philippines, China, and Australia have integrated the pink meat into their local cuisines. Perhaps SPAM is most famous for feeding the world during and after World War II. In <strong>Britain</strong>, SPAM is so popular they have an annual SPAM Week and <strong>Monty Python</strong> had success on Broadway with the <strong><em>Spamalot</em></strong> musical. More SPAM is consumed per person in <strong>Hawaii</strong>&nbsp;than in any other state, and every year since 2002 they have held the <a href="http://www.spamjamhawaii.com"><strong>Waikiki SPAM JAM®</strong></a>. There was no mention in the museum of Austin, Texas’ <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spamarama"><strong>SPAMarama</strong></a> that ran from 1978 to 2007 and was resurrected in 2019 and 2022.&nbsp;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>The SPAM</em>® <em>Museum is at 101 Third Ave. NE, Austin, Minn. Admission is free. The museum and attached gift shop are open daily. Austin, Minn, is 1,104 miles almost due north of us, 30 miles off I-35.</em></p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Travel Notes:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" width="780" height="520" data-attachment-id="466854" data-permalink="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-beyond-september-events-roundup/attachment/sept3_bolivar-lighthouse/" data-orig-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?fit=1800%2C1200&amp;ssl=1" data-orig-size="1800,1200" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="Sept3_Bolivar Lighthouse" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;For over 150 years the Bolivar Point Lighthouse has greeted visitors making the crossing from Galveston to the peninsula.&lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?fit=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1" src="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=780%2C520&#038;ssl=1" alt="" class="wp-image-466854" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1 1024w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=600%2C400&amp;ssl=1 600w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=768%2C512&amp;ssl=1 768w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=1536%2C1024&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=1200%2C800&amp;ssl=1 1200w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=550%2C367&amp;ssl=1 550w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=800%2C533&amp;ssl=1 800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1 780w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?resize=400%2C267&amp;ssl=1 400w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse.jpg?w=1800&amp;ssl=1 1800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept3_Bolivar-Lighthouse-1024x683.jpg?w=370&amp;ssl=1 370w" sizes="(max-width: 780px) 100vw, 780px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">For over 150 years the Bolivar Point Lighthouse has greeted visitors making the crossing from Galveston to the peninsula. <span class="image-credit"><span class="credit-label-wrapper">Credit:</span> Gerald E. McLeod</span></figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>See the light.</strong> On Sept. 13, 2008, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hurricane_Ike"><strong>Hurricane Ike</strong></a> devastated <strong>Galveston</strong> and inundated the Bolivar Peninsula. Hammered by sustained winds of 110 mph, the ironclad <a href="https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/bolivar-lighthouse"><strong>Bolivar Point Lighthouse</strong></a> stood strong. It wasn’t the 117-foot tower’s first hurricane. First lit in 1872, the lighthouse has survived several hurricanes, tropical storms, and the catastrophic <strong>Hurricane of 1900</strong> when 125 refugees sheltered in its walls. Decommissioned in 1933 and sold at auction in 1947, the lighthouse is now being restored by the nonprofit <a href="https://bolivarpointlighthouse.org/"><strong>Bolivar Point Lighthouse Foundation</strong></a><strong>.</strong> On <strong>Sept. 13</strong>, the foundation invites visitors to tour the lighthouse from 10am to 2pm and celebrate the resilience of the peninsula on the anniversary of Hurricane Ike.&nbsp;</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" width="360" height="198" data-attachment-id="466855" data-permalink="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-beyond-september-events-roundup/attachment/sept4_neches-river/" data-orig-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept4_Neches-River.jpg?fit=360%2C198&amp;ssl=1" data-orig-size="360,198" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="Sept4_Neches River" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;A new documentary film introduces viewers to the Neches River.  &lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept4_Neches-River.jpg?fit=360%2C198&amp;ssl=1" src="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept4_Neches-River.jpg?resize=360%2C198&#038;ssl=1" alt="" class="wp-image-466855" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept4_Neches-River.jpg?w=360&amp;ssl=1 360w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept4_Neches-River.jpg?w=370&amp;ssl=1 370w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept4_Neches-River.jpg?w=400&amp;ssl=1 400w" sizes="(max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">A new documentary film introduces viewers to the Neches River. &nbsp;</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Wild and free.</strong> The one-hour documentary film <a href="https://www.nechesriverfilm.com/watch" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">The Neches River: Wild Heart of East Texas</a> follows the people who live along the Neches – those who study it, enjoy it, and work to protect it&nbsp;– offering an intimate look at a river and a way of life that most Texans have never encountered. From its source in <strong>Van Zandt County</strong>&nbsp;the Neches flows 416 miles&nbsp;through the&nbsp;<strong>Piney Woods</strong>&nbsp;of East&nbsp;Texas, defining the boundaries of 14 counties on its way to its mouth on&nbsp;<strong>Sabine Lake</strong>&nbsp;near the&nbsp;<strong>Rainbow Bridge</strong>. Except for dams and man-made lakes, much of the river is in a natural state flowing through national wildlife refuges and the <strong>Davy Crockett National Forest</strong>. Although the Neches is recognized as one of the last remaining ecosystems of its kind in the U.S., its beauty and ecological importance remain largely hidden to most. The hourlong film by producer <strong>Ellen Temple</strong> and cinematographer <strong>Curtis Craven</strong> is available to stream online for <a href="https://www.nechesriverfilm.com/watch"><strong>free</strong></a>.&nbsp;</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" width="780" height="585" data-attachment-id="466857" data-permalink="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-beyond-september-events-roundup/attachment/olympus-digital-camera-32/" data-orig-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?fit=1570%2C1178&amp;ssl=1" data-orig-size="1570,1178" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;A short distance south of Austin, the Duchman Family Winery sits among the hills and woodlands outside of Driftwood. &lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?fit=780%2C585&amp;ssl=1" src="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=780%2C585&#038;ssl=1" alt="" class="wp-image-466857" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=1024%2C768&amp;ssl=1 1024w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=600%2C450&amp;ssl=1 600w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=768%2C576&amp;ssl=1 768w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=1536%2C1152&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=1200%2C900&amp;ssl=1 1200w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=800%2C600&amp;ssl=1 800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=400%2C300&amp;ssl=1 400w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=200%2C150&amp;ssl=1 200w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=550%2C413&amp;ssl=1 550w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?resize=780%2C585&amp;ssl=1 780w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery.jpg?w=1570&amp;ssl=1 1570w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept5_Duchman-Winery-1024x768.jpg?w=370&amp;ssl=1 370w" sizes="(max-width: 780px) 100vw, 780px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">A short distance south of Austin, the Duchman Family Winery sits among the hills and woodlands outside of Driftwood.  <span class="image-credit"><span class="credit-label-wrapper">Credit:</span> Gerald E. McLeod</span></figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Wine time.</strong> Congratulations to <a href="https://www.duchmanwinery.com/"><strong>Duchman Family Winery</strong></a> in Driftwood for being selected by <strong>USA Today</strong> as No. 3 on its list of <a href="https://10best.usatoday.com/awards/best-wine-tasting-room/">10 best wine tasting rooms across the U.S.</a> That is quite a feat when you consider it ranked above established wineries in California and Oregon. Founded in 2004, the winery uses <strong>Texas-grown grapes</strong> for its award-winning Vermentino, Aglianico, Tempranillo, and Roussanne wines. The scenic winery also has the advantage of being next door to the wonderful <a href="https://trattorialisina.com/"><strong>Trattoria Lisina Italian Restaurant</strong></a>.&nbsp;</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img data-recalc-dims="1" decoding="async" width="780" height="520" data-attachment-id="466858" data-permalink="https://www.austinchronicle.com/columns/day-trips-beyond-september-events-roundup/attachment/sept6_guadalupe-sp/" data-orig-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?fit=1800%2C1200&amp;ssl=1" data-orig-size="1800,1200" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-title="Sept6_Guadalupe SP" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;TPWD has opened a 3.5-mile hiking trail at Honey Creek State Natural Area, which shares river frontage with Guadalupe State Park. &lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?fit=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1" src="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=780%2C520&#038;ssl=1" alt="" class="wp-image-466858" srcset="https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=1024%2C683&amp;ssl=1 1024w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=600%2C400&amp;ssl=1 600w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=768%2C512&amp;ssl=1 768w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=1536%2C1024&amp;ssl=1 1536w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=1200%2C800&amp;ssl=1 1200w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=550%2C367&amp;ssl=1 550w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=800%2C533&amp;ssl=1 800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=780%2C520&amp;ssl=1 780w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?resize=400%2C267&amp;ssl=1 400w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP.jpg?w=1800&amp;ssl=1 1800w, https://i0.wp.com/www.austinchronicle.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Sept6_Guadalupe-SP-1024x683.jpg?w=370&amp;ssl=1 370w" sizes="(max-width: 780px) 100vw, 780px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">TPWD has opened a 3.5-mile hiking trail at Honey Creek State Natural Area, which shares river frontage with Guadalupe State Park.  <span class="image-credit"><span class="credit-label-wrapper">Credit:</span> Gerald E. McLeod</span></figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Sweet spot.</strong> For years the fragile environment of <a href="https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/honey-creek"><strong>Honey Creek State Natural Area</strong></a> has been off-limits to the public except by guided tour. Beginning this summer <a href="https://tpwd.texas.gov/newsmedia/releases/?req=20260807a&amp;utm_campaign=govdelivery-email&amp;utm_medium=email&amp;utm_source=govdelivery"><strong>Texas Parks &amp; Wildlife Department</strong></a> has opened a 3.5-mile hiking trail for public day use without a park guide. Visitors will need to obtain a reservation through the TPWD <a href="https://texasstateparks.reserveamerica.com/?pk_vid=e0c501d6761784b617883047280250ef"><strong>website</strong></a>, through the call center (<strong>512/389-8900)</strong>, or a QR code on-site. Directions and gate codes are provided to those with reservations. The natural area is adjacent to <a href="https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/guadalupe-river"><strong>Guadalupe River State Park</strong></a> and shares some of the same river frontage. <a href="https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/honey-creek/park_events"><strong>Guided hikes</strong></a> are still being offered on Saturday and Sunday at 8:30am. Honey Creek SNA encompasses 2,809 acres in western <strong>Comal County</strong>, about 30 miles north of downtown <strong>San Antonio</strong>. Much of the land was part of a homestead by German settlers beginning in 1866. The state acquired the property in 1985 through the <a href="https://www.nature.org/en-us/about-us/where-we-work/united-states/texas/"><strong>Texas Nature Conservancy</strong></a>.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Other September Events:</strong></h2>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>BIG BEND&nbsp;</strong></h2>



<div class="wp-block-group is-vertical is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-4fc3f8e1 wp-block-group-is-layout-flex">
